{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on b7281a4e: yes. Fresh CLI discovery stays, and JP1 uses\nthe pending-enrollment path for the other-service row.\n\n1. A fresh `dap join` has no service of its own, so the link names\n   the service. JP1 does not change that. It is a stated\n   difference between the surfaces and not a gap: parity means the\n   same answer for the same link and the same expected service.\n\n2. The shared function in packages/client takes the link text and\n   an expected service that may be absent. With a service, it\n   refuses a link for another service. With none, it returns the\n   service of the link. The page and MCP always pass one. The CLI\n   passes `before.service` when a config exists and none when the\n   directory is fresh.\n\n3. The table runs the CLI rows through the actual\n   pending-enrollment path over TEST memory custody, as you\n   propose. Add one row for the fresh CLI: a valid link with no\n   config is accepted, and the saved service is the normalized\n   service of the link. The table then has six rows, and the sixth\n   applies to the CLI only. Say so in the test's name.\n\n4. Normalize the trailing slash once, in the shared function,\n   before the pending comparison and before the link digest. The\n   exact saved intent, secret and receipt checks do not change. A\n   join left pending by an earlier build with a slash in its saved\n   service then fails the comparison. Accept that: state it in the\n   implementation note in one sentence, and add no migration.\n\n5. No new flag. The tagged receivers and the check mode stay out\n   of JP1.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}